      "text": "# Least Recently Used (LRU) Cache in Python\n\n## Overview\n\nAn LRU cache evicts the least recently used item when it reaches its capacity. The classic efficient implementation combines a **hash map** (for O(1) lookups) with a **doubly linked list** (for O(1) ordering).\n\n## Complete Implementation\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Any, Optional\n\n\nclass Node:\n    \"\"\"A node in the doubly linked list.\n\n    Each node stores a key-value pair along with pointers to its\n    neighbors, allowing constant-time insertion and removal.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    __slots__ = (\"key\", \"value\", \"prev\", \"next\")  # saves memory\n\n    def __init__(self, key: Any = None, value: Any = None) -> None:\n        self.key = key\n        self.value = value\n        self.prev: Optional[\"Node\"] = None\n        self.next: Optional[\"Node\"] = None\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"A fixed-capacity cache that evicts the least recently used entry.\n\n    The cache maintains two data structures working in tandem:\n      - A dict mapping keys to nodes for O(1) lookup.\n",
